# SupremeOS native-linux — Casambi Cloud fleet-wide default account (§ Casambi fleet-wide
# env-var default).
#
# THIS FILE ITSELF IS AN EXAMPLE — it ships in the git repo with blank values and is never
# read directly. Copy it to a real, machine-local, NEVER-git-tracked location before filling
# in real values:
#
#   sudo cp config/casambi-fleet-credentials.example /etc/supremeos/casambi-fleet-credentials
#   sudo nano /etc/supremeos/casambi-fleet-credentials   # fill in real values
#   sudo chown root:root /etc/supremeos/casambi-fleet-credentials
#   sudo chmod 0600 /etc/supremeos/casambi-fleet-credentials
#
# Once that file exists (mode 0600, root-owned), BOTH of these pick it up automatically —
# no env var to export, no flag to remember:
#   - install.sh, on a brand-new machine (collect_answers() checks for this exact path before
#     falling back to already-exported env vars / blank — see that function's own comment).
#   - apply-casambi-credentials.sh, on an ALREADY-installed hub — rewrites the 4 keys into the
#     existing /etc/supremeos/install.conf, re-renders gateway.env, restarts supreme-gateway.
#     Safe to re-run any time this file's values change (e.g. rotating the password).
#
# Format: same simple VARNAME="value" lines install.conf itself uses — no command
# substitution, no `$()`, no backticks, no `;`/`&`/`|` (rejected the same way
# install.sh's load_install_conf_safely() rejects them in install.conf).
#
# SUPREME_CASAMBI_NETWORK_ID is NOT part of the fleet-wide account — it pins which Casambi
# network THIS specific hub's fixtures live in under that account, which genuinely varies per
# installation. Leave it blank here unless every hub this file gets copied to really does
# share the exact same single Casambi network.

SUPREME_CASAMBI_API_KEY=""
SUPREME_CASAMBI_EMAIL=""
SUPREME_CASAMBI_PASSWORD=""
SUPREME_CASAMBI_NETWORK_ID=""
